// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["xix-a"] = {"qs": [["Taxation – India Specific", "A non-resident investor in a Category II AIF wants to claim lower tax under a tax treaty. She typically needs to provide:", ["Only her passport copy", "A tax residency certificate and documents", "Nothing, since non-residents are exempt", "Her Indian bank fixed deposit receipts"], 1, "Treaty benefits require a tax residency certificate and related documentation; the AIF may then apply the treaty rate on income allocable to her."], ["Valuation", "A company has EBITDA of ₹30 crore and comparable companies trade at 8x EV/EBITDA. If net debt is ₹60 crore, the equity value is:", ["₹240 crore", "₹180 crore", "₹300 crore", "₹210 crore"], 1, "Enterprise value = 30 × 8 = ₹240 crore. Equity value = EV − net debt = 240 − 60 = ₹180 crore. ₹240 crore is the enterprise value, not equity."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "A close-ended AIF's tenure has ended but some unlisted holdings remain unsold. One option within SEBI's framework is to:", ["Convert the scheme into an open-ended mutual fund", "Extend the tenure indefinitely without investor approval", "Distribute the holdings in specie to investors who agree", "Sell the holdings to the sponsor at book value without consent"], 2, "SEBI permits in-specie distribution of unliquidated investments with investor approval, or use of the dissolution route. Indefinite extension or forced sales to the sponsor are not allowed."], ["Valuation", "Which valuation approach is most suitable for an infrastructure asset with long, predictable contracted cash flows?", ["Price of the most recent funding round", "Discounted cash flow", "Liquidation value", "Book value of share capital"], 1, "Predictable long-term cash flows suit DCF valuation. Recent round prices fit early-stage companies, and liquidation value suits distressed situations."], ["Risk and Return – Fund and Investor Perspective", "A fund uses a subscription credit line to fund deals before calling capital from investors. This tends to:", ["Reduce the fund's total profits automatically", "Raise reported IRR by delaying capital calls", "Lower the reported IRR", "Eliminate the J-curve and all fees"], 1, "Delaying capital calls shortens the period investor money is at work, boosting IRR, though interest costs may reduce the absolute multiple. Investors should look at both measures."], ["AIF Regulatory Framework", "Which statement about accredited investors in AIFs is correct?", ["They can redeem units of close-ended Category II AIFs on demand at NAV", "They may invest below the ₹1 crore minimum", "They must invest at least ₹10 crore in every scheme", "They are exempt from KYC requirements"], 1, "Accredited investors are exempt from the ₹1 crore minimum investment requirement and can access accredited-investor-only schemes. They are still subject to KYC and fund lock-ins."], ["Good Practices", "A client wants to invest in an AIF through the direct plan after receiving advice from a SEBI-registered investment adviser. The distributor should:", ["Insist that the client invests through the regular plan", "Charge the full distribution fee anyway", "Refuse to process the client's KYC", "Not claim distribution fee on that investment"], 3, "Direct plans carry no distribution fee; investors advised by registered investment advisers may use them. Pushing a regular plan for a fee would be improper."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "Why do investors look at a manager's portfolio construction plan during due diligence?", ["To calculate the investor's own tax liability", "To verify the custodian's net worth", "To set the investor's KYC risk category", "To check if diversification and sizing fit the strategy"], 3, "The plan shows how many companies the fund will hold, typical ticket sizes and reserves for follow-ons, which determine risk and consistency with the stated strategy."], ["AIF Regulatory Framework", "A Category I AIF has a corpus of ₹80 crore. The minimum continuing interest required from the sponsor or manager is:", ["₹5 crore", "₹4 crore", "₹80 lakh", "₹2 crore"], 3, "For Category I and II AIFs, continuing interest is the lower of 2.5% of corpus or ₹5 crore. 2.5% of 80 crore = ₹2 crore, which is lower than ₹5 crore."], ["Alternative Investment Fund Structuring", "Which party in a trust-based AIF is primarily responsible for ensuring that the manager acts in accordance with the trust deed and PPM?", ["The trustee", "The placement agent", "The investee company", "The tax consultant"], 0, "The trustee oversees the fund on behalf of beneficiaries and ensures the manager follows the trust deed and PPM. Placement agents only help raise capital."], ["Alternative Investment Funds in India", "Category I AIFs are distinguished mainly because they invest in sectors that:", ["Have the highest expected returns", "Are listed on recognised stock exchanges", "Government or regulators view as socially or economically desirable", "Require leverage to generate returns"], 2, "Category I covers funds investing in start-ups, SMEs, social ventures, infrastructure and special situations, which policymakers consider desirable and may support with incentives."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "Which document shows an individual investor's contributions, distributions and share of NAV in an AIF?", ["Capital account statement", "Private placement memorandum", "Trust deed", "Investment management agreement"], 0, "A capital account statement tracks each investor's commitment, amounts called, distributions and current value. The PPM and trust deed are constitutional documents."], ["Concepts in the AIF Industry", "A co-investment opportunity in a private fund context means:", ["The fund invests in another AIF of the same manager", "Two managers jointly manage a single scheme", "An investor transfers its commitment to another investor at a discount", "Investors invest directly alongside the fund in a specific deal"], 3, "Co-investment lets fund investors take an additional direct stake in a particular portfolio company alongside the AIF, often at lower or no fees. Transferring a commitment is a secondary sale."], ["Good Practices", "Why does SEBI restrict upfront distribution commissions for Category I and II AIFs?", ["To increase the distributor's total income", "To guarantee higher returns to investors", "To reduce incentives for churning and mis-selling", "To allow early redemption"], 2, "Spreading commission as trail over the fund's life aligns the distributor's income with the investor's holding and reduces incentives to push products for upfront fees."], ["Risk and Return – Fund and Investor Perspective", "Why can IRR alone be misleading when comparing private funds?", ["IRR ignores the timing of cash flows and so is unaffected by early exits", "IRR cannot be calculated for close-ended funds", "A quick exit with small gains can show a high IRR", "IRR is always lower than the money multiple"], 2, "IRR is sensitive to timing: a quick exit with modest gains can produce a high IRR but little absolute wealth. Investors therefore look at multiples alongside IRR."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "A manager refuses to share the names of the independent valuer and the fund's auditor during due diligence. This is:", ["A red flag warranting further enquiry", "Normal practice to protect business secrets of the fund manager from competitors", "Irrelevant to operational risk", "Required by SEBI confidentiality rules"], 0, "Transparency about key service providers is basic; refusal to disclose them raises concerns about valuation and controls. No regulation requires hiding them from investors."], ["Taxation – India Specific", "When a Category I or II AIF distributes income (other than business income) to a resident investor, the fund generally:", ["Pays no attention to tax because income is exempt", "Pays the final tax on behalf of all investors at the maximum marginal rate", "Deducts GST from the distribution", "Deducts tax at source as prescribed"], 3, "The AIF deducts TDS on income credited or paid to investors, as prescribed by law; investors then pay tax on their share, claiming credit for TDS. GST does not apply to distributions."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "A PE fund sells its stake in a portfolio company to another PE fund. This exit route is called a:", ["Initial public offering", "Buyback by the promoter", "Secondary sale", "Write-off"], 2, "Selling to another financial investor is a secondary sale. An IPO lists the shares, and a buyback involves the company or promoters repurchasing them."], ["Risk and Return – Fund and Investor Perspective", "An investor commits to private equity funds of five different vintage years. The main benefit is:", ["Reducing exposure to one year's entry valuations", "Avoiding management fees in later years", "Guaranteeing a positive IRR in each fund", "Shortening the lock-in of each fund"], 0, "Vintage diversification spreads capital across different market and valuation conditions, reducing the risk of investing all at a market peak."], ["AIF Regulatory Framework", "Category I and II AIFs may borrow only for temporary funding needs. Which condition is part of this framework?", ["Borrowing is capped as a share of investable funds and for a short period", "Borrowing is unlimited if investors approve", "Borrowing is permitted only to buy listed shares", "Borrowings must be at least 50% of the corpus"], 0, "SEBI permits such AIFs to borrow only to meet temporary shortfalls, within limits on amount, duration and frequency. Leverage for investment is not allowed for these categories."], ["Taxation – India Specific", "Unlike Category I and II AIFs, a Category III AIF set up as a trust is generally taxed:", ["Only in investors' hands with full pass-through for all types of income", "At zero rate because it invests in listed securities", "Only on unrealised gains", "At the fund level, as no statutory pass-through applies"], 3, "Category III AIFs do not enjoy statutory pass-through, so income is generally taxed at the trust level under trust-taxation rules."], ["Concepts in the AIF Industry", "'Gross IRR' and 'net IRR' of an AIF differ mainly because net IRR:", ["Includes only realised investments and ignores unrealised holdings", "Deducts fees, expenses and carried interest", "Is calculated before management fees", "Uses the hurdle rate instead of actual cash flows"], 1, "Net IRR reflects the investor's actual experience after fees, fund expenses and carry. Gross IRR measures portfolio performance before these costs."], ["Risk and Return – Fund and Investor Perspective", "The J-curve suggests that an investor reviewing a fund in year two should:", ["Immediately sell the interest in the secondary market at any price", "Expect the highest returns in the first year", "Ignore fees because they do not affect early returns", "Not conclude that early negative returns mean failure"], 3, "Early returns are depressed by fees and costs before investments mature, so negative early IRRs are normal. Fees are a key driver of the J-curve's dip."], ["Alternative Investment Fund Structuring", "A key reason most Indian AIFs are structured as trusts is that trusts:", ["Are the only structure permitted by SEBI for AIFs", "Pay no tax under any circumstances", "Are flexible to set up and wind up", "Allow unlimited leverage"], 2, "Trusts are flexible to establish and dissolve and fit the statutory pass-through regime for Category I and II AIFs. Companies and LLPs are also permitted, and trusts are not tax-free in all cases."], ["Concepts in the AIF Industry", "A 'first close' in fundraising means that:", ["The fund has stopped accepting all new investors permanently", "The fund has accepted initial commitments and can begin operating", "All capital has been drawn down", "The fund's first investment has been exited"], 1, "At first close, the fund admits its initial investors and can start investing, while it may continue raising money until the final close."], ["Risk and Return – Fund and Investor Perspective", "The public market equivalent (PME) method helps an investor answer:", ["What the fund's management fee should be", "Whether the fund is legally registered", "Whether the fund beat a comparable public index", "How many investors the fund has"], 2, "PME compares a private fund's cash flows with a hypothetical investment in a public index made at the same times, showing relative outperformance."], ["Valuation", "A fund continues to value a start-up at its last round price two years after investment, though the company has missed every milestone. This is a concern because:", ["Last-round price is never allowed in any valuation", "The valuer is required to use book value", "Missed milestones always increase value", "Stale values overstate NAV and performance"], 3, "Valuations must be updated for developments; keeping an outdated round price after poor performance overstates value and can mislead investors."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "After investing, a PE fund typically monitors portfolio companies by:", ["Board seats, regular MIS and covenant tracking", "Selling the shares within three months of purchase", "Relying only on annual audited accounts", "Leaving management entirely unsupervised"], 0, "Active monitoring through board representation, periodic reports and covenant checks lets the fund protect value and intervene early. Annual accounts alone are too infrequent."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiation – Investor Perspective", "An investor advisory committee (IAC) of an AIF commonly:", ["Makes all investment decisions instead of the manager", "Sets the investors' tax rates", "Appoints the SEBI inspection team", "Reviews conflicts and valuation issues"], 3, "The IAC, made up of investor representatives, advises on conflicts of interest, valuation questions and certain consents. Investment decisions remain with the manager."], ["Risk and Return – Fund and Investor Perspective", "Which risk is most significant for an Indian investor in an AIF that invests in a few large unlisted companies?", ["Interest-rate risk on government bonds held as cash management", "Inflation-indexation risk", "Exchange-rate risk on rupee-denominated holdings", "Concentration risk"], 3, "A small number of large holdings makes outcomes depend on a few companies. Rupee-denominated holdings carry no exchange-rate risk for a domestic investor."], ["Good Practices", "Before recommending a Category II AIF, a distributor should explain to the client that:", ["The client can redeem at NAV any time", "Returns are assured by the sponsor", "The money will be locked in for years", "The fund is regulated as a mutual fund"], 2, "Category II AIFs are close-ended and illiquid; investors must understand the lock-in and drawdown commitments. Returns are not assured and AIFs are not mutual funds."], ["Good Practices", "An AIF distributor's KYC and AML obligations include:", ["Accepting cash in large amounts to speed onboarding", "Ignoring politically exposed persons", "Skipping KYC for accredited investors", "Verifying identity and source of funds"], 3, "AML rules require verifying identity, beneficial ownership and source of funds, with enhanced checks for higher-risk clients like PEPs. Accredited investors still need KYC."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "The annual audit of compliance with the terms of the PPM is meant to:", ["Verify the scheme's adherence to its PPM", "Replace the statutory audit of accounts", "Value each portfolio company", "Calculate carried interest"], 0, "The PPM audit checks whether the scheme has followed its stated terms and disclosures. It complements, but does not replace, the statutory financial audit or valuations."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "Reference checks with founders of companies previously backed by the manager help an investor assess:", ["The manager's behaviour as a partner", "The fund's tax status", "The legal validity of the trust deed", "The SEBI registration number"], 0, "Speaking with founders and co-investors reveals how the manager behaves, adds value and handles difficulties — information that documents cannot provide."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "Which characteristic is most typical of alternative investments compared with listed large-cap shares?", ["Higher minimum ticket sizes", "Daily pricing on a recognised stock exchange with continuous liquidity", "Lower fees because managers are passive", "Full transparency of holdings every day"], 0, "Alternatives usually require large minimum investments, are illiquid and charge higher, more complex fees. Daily exchange pricing and full transparency are features of listed securities."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "Which of these best indicates alignment of interest between a manager and investors?", ["High management fees charged on committed capital throughout the fund's entire life", "A guaranteed minimum carry for the manager", "Meaningful team investment and carry paid after capital is returned", "Carry paid deal by deal with no clawback"], 2, "Personal capital at risk and a whole-of-fund waterfall tie the manager's rewards to investors' outcomes. High fees and guaranteed carry weaken alignment."], ["Good Practices", "Which of the following must a distributor disclose to the investor?", ["Personal details of other clients", "The distribution fee earned from the AIF", "The manager's internal deal pipeline", "The SEBI inspection reports of other distributors"], 1, "Distributors must disclose commissions and fees they receive from the AIF so the investor understands potential conflicts. Other clients' information must remain confidential."], ["Alternative Investment Funds in India", "The rapid growth of commitments raised by AIFs in India over the last decade is best explained by:", ["A legal requirement for banks to invest a fixed share of deposits in AIFs", "Rising HNI wealth and demand for private-market access", "AIFs guaranteeing higher returns than fixed deposits", "Mutual funds being banned from equity investing"], 1, "AIF growth reflects growing wealth, demand for private-market exposure and institutional interest. AIFs do not guarantee returns, and there is no deposit-investment mandate for banks."], ["AIF Regulatory Framework", "A new AIF scheme must file its placement memorandum with SEBI through:", ["The fund's statutory auditor", "A SEBI-registered merchant banker", "A stock exchange", "The custodian of the AIF"], 1, "SEBI requires the PPM of a new scheme to be filed through a SEBI-registered merchant banker, who carries out due diligence on the disclosures."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "An investor learns that the fund manager's chief financial officer also acts as the fund's sole valuation decision-maker without independent review. The main concern is:", ["Excessive diversification of portfolio holdings", "Lack of independence in valuation", "Too many service providers", "High distribution fees"], 1, "Valuations drive reported performance and fees. Concentrating valuation decisions in management without independent review creates conflicts and the risk of inflated values."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "An investor sees that a private equity fund's quarterly reported returns have a very low standard deviation. A key caution is that:", ["Smoothed, appraisal-based valuations can understate true volatility", "Low volatility proves the fund carries no market risk at all and can replace debt holdings", "Private equity is always less risky than government bonds", "Reported volatility of private funds is audited daily"], 0, "Private fund valuations are periodic and judgement-based, which smooths returns and understates risk. Low reported volatility does not mean the absence of market risk."], ["Valuation", "A PE fund holds preference shares with a 1x liquidation preference in a company that may be sold at a modest valuation. The valuation should:", ["Reflect the preference's effect on proceeds", "Ignore the preference and value all shares equally", "Assume the preference will never be used", "Use only the par value of preference shares"], 0, "Liquidation preferences change how sale proceeds are distributed, especially at lower exit values, so they must be reflected in fair value."], ["Concepts in the AIF Industry", "A fund's investors must receive their capital plus an 8% annual compounded return before carry is paid. On ₹100 crore held for three years, the amount investors must receive first is about:", ["₹124.00 crore", "₹108.00 crore", "₹133.10 crore", "₹125.97 crore"], 3, "With compounding, 100 × 1.08³ ≈ ₹125.97 crore. ₹124 crore uses simple interest, and ₹133.10 crore uses 10% instead of 8%."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "In the investment process of a PE fund, 'screening' refers to:", ["The detailed legal review of the share purchase agreement", "An initial filter of deals against the fund's strategy", "The final exit of a portfolio company", "Valuation of the fund's units for investors"], 1, "Screening is a quick initial assessment of many opportunities against the strategy, size and sector criteria before detailed due diligence on the few that pass."], ["Good Practices", "A distributor tells a client that an AIF is 'SEBI-approved, so it cannot lose money'. This is:", ["Misleading, as registration is not a guarantee", "Accurate, as SEBI guarantees AIF returns", "Acceptable if the client is an HNI", "Accurate for Category I funds only"], 0, "SEBI registration means the fund is regulated, not that its returns are protected. Claiming otherwise is mis-selling."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "For which investor is a meaningful allocation to alternative investments most suitable?", ["A wealthy family with long horizon and surplus liquidity", "A retiree who needs monthly income from a small corpus and may need cash for medical bills", "A young saver building an emergency fund", "A trader who needs daily access to all funds"], 0, "Alternatives suit investors with long horizons, large wealth and no need for near-term liquidity. Those needing regular income or liquidity are poorly matched to locked-in products."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiation – Investor Perspective", "Why do investors often negotiate a cap on organisational (set-up) expenses?", ["To prevent the fund from being registered with SEBI", "To limit fund costs borne by investors", "To ensure the fund invests only in listed shares", "To avoid paying any management fee at all during the fund's life"], 1, "Set-up costs such as legal and registration fees are charged to the fund; a cap prevents excessive costs eroding investor returns."], ["Valuation", "A company is expected to produce free cash flow of ₹12 crore next year, growing at 4% forever. At a 14% discount rate, its enterprise value is:", ["₹85.7 crore", "₹120 crore", "₹300 crore", "₹66.7 crore"], 1, "Using the growing perpetuity formula, EV = 12 ÷ (0.14 − 0.04) = ₹120 crore. ₹85.7 crore ignores growth (12 ÷ 0.14)."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "A member of an AIF's investment committee has a personal stake in a target company. Good governance requires that member to:", ["Vote in favour to support the company", "Keep the interest confidential to avoid delays", "Disclose the interest and recuse from the decision", "Resign from the manager immediately in every case"], 2, "Conflicted members should disclose their interest and abstain from deliberation and voting. Resignation is not normally required, but secrecy breaches governance norms."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "An AIF manager allocates a hot deal only to a fund where it earns higher carry, ignoring another fund with the same mandate. This breaches:", ["The duty of fair allocation among funds", "Concentration limits only", "Reporting timelines", "Stewardship code voting rules"], 0, "Managers must allocate opportunities fairly as per a disclosed allocation policy, not based on their own economics. Concentration limits and voting rules are separate matters."], ["Concepts in the AIF Industry", "An investor committed ₹5 crore to a Category II AIF and 35% has been drawn down so far. The uncalled commitment is:", ["₹1.75 crore", "₹3.25 crore", "₹5.00 crore", "₹3.50 crore"], 1, "Drawn amount = 35% × 5 crore = ₹1.75 crore. Uncalled = 5 − 1.75 = ₹3.25 crore. ₹1.75 crore is the amount already called."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "An AIF must report periodically to SEBI. A key purpose of such reporting is to allow SEBI to:", ["Approve each individual investment in advance", "Calculate investors' income tax", "Set the fund's management fee", "Monitor the industry's investments and risks"], 3, "Periodic reporting enables SEBI to oversee activity, leverage, concentration and compliance across AIFs. SEBI does not pre-approve investments or set fees."], ["Risk and Return – Fund and Investor Perspective", "A fund has paid-in capital of ₹80 crore, distributions of ₹60 crore and residual NAV of ₹52 crore. Its TVPI is:", ["1.40x", "0.75x", "0.65x", "1.75x"], 0, "TVPI = (distributions + NAV) ÷ paid-in = (60 + 52) ÷ 80 = 1.40x. 0.75x is the DPI (60 ÷ 80) and 0.65x is the RVPI (52 ÷ 80)."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "Which entity in a trust-based AIF is responsible for monitoring the manager's compliance with the PPM and regulations?", ["The trustee, supported by the compliance officer", "The investee companies", "The placement agent", "The largest investor alone"], 0, "The trustee oversees the manager, and the manager's compliance officer monitors adherence to regulations and the PPM. Investee companies and agents have no oversight role."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiation – Investor Perspective", "Which expense is most likely to be negotiated as borne by the manager rather than the fund?", ["Fund audit fees", "Custodian charges", "Legal costs of closing portfolio deals", "Manager's own office rent and salaries"], 3, "Overheads such as the manager's office and staff are usually paid out of the management fee. Fund-level expenses like audit, custody and deal costs are commonly charged to the fund."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "A first-time fund is led by professionals who spun out of a large established PE firm. The investor's key diligence focus should be:", ["Whether the old firm's brand is mentioned in the PPM and on the new manager's website", "The size of the new office", "The number of LinkedIn followers of the partners", "Whether they can operate their own firm"], 3, "Spin-out teams may have good deal records but must show they can run a firm — operations, compliance, fundraising and team stability. Branding and office size are not meaningful."], ["Taxation – India Specific", "A Category II AIF incurs a capital loss on an investment. The loss is generally:", ["Retained at fund level forever", "Passed through to investors who meet holding conditions", "Converted into business loss", "Ignored for tax purposes"], 1, "Losses other than business losses are passed to investors, provided they have held units for the prescribed minimum period. Business losses stay at fund level."], ["Alternative Investment Fund Structuring", "Which document appoints the investment manager of a trust-based AIF and sets out its duties and fees?", ["The trust deed alone without any separate agreement with the manager", "The contribution agreement", "The custodian's agreement", "The investment management agreement"], 3, "The trustee appoints the manager through an investment management agreement, which specifies powers, duties and fees. The contribution agreement governs investors' commitments."], ["Alternative Investment Fund Structuring", "If an Indian AIF's sponsor and manager are owned and controlled by resident Indian citizens, its downstream investment is treated under FEMA as:", ["Foreign direct investment in all cases", "Foreign portfolio investment", "Domestic investment, even if some investors are foreign", "Not permitted at all"], 2, "Under FEMA, downstream investments by an AIF are treated as foreign investment only if the sponsor or manager is not owned and controlled by resident Indian citizens. Foreign LP money alone does not change this."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "Why is the dispersion of returns between top and bottom managers usually wider in private equity than in large-cap equity mutual funds?", ["Private equity managers are not allowed to diversify across companies at all", "All private equity funds track the same index", "Outcomes depend heavily on manager skill in sourcing and value creation", "Regulators fix the returns of private equity funds"], 2, "Private equity returns depend on deal sourcing, negotiation and active value creation, so manager selection matters greatly. Listed large-cap funds hold similar stocks and their returns cluster more closely."], ["Good Practices", "Which situation suggests possible mis-selling of an AIF?", ["A family office with large surplus invests 5% of its portfolio", "An accredited investor invests after reading the PPM", "A client nearing retirement invests most of her savings in one illiquid fund", "A business owner commits after independent advice"], 2, "Putting most of a near-retiree's savings into one illiquid fund ignores liquidity needs and diversification, suggesting unsuitable advice."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiation – Investor Perspective", "Which clause limits an investor's ability to sell its fund interest to a third party?", ["Most favoured nation clause", "Key-person clause", "Hurdle rate clause", "Transfer restrictions requiring manager consent"], 3, "Transfer restriction clauses require the manager's consent before an investor sells its interest, protecting the fund from unsuitable investors."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "Which of the following is a real asset rather than a financial claim?", ["A corporate bond issued by a toll road company", "A toll road owned by an infrastructure fund", "A listed share of an infrastructure company", "Units of a liquid mutual fund that holds treasury bills"], 1, "A toll road is a tangible, real asset. Bonds, shares and fund units are financial claims, even if they relate to infrastructure companies."], ["Alternative Investment Fund Structuring", "Why might an AIF choose an LLP structure instead of a trust?", ["LLPs need no registration with SEBI to operate as AIFs", "Partners' liability is limited", "LLPs are exempt from FEMA rules", "LLPs are the only structure for Category III funds"], 1, "An LLP gives partners limited liability and a contractual framework familiar to global investors. It must still register with SEBI as an AIF and follow FEMA."], ["Alternative Investment Funds in India", "A fund that invests in listed and unlisted securities of stressed companies, including through acquisition of stressed loans, falls in which sub-category?", ["Special situation fund under Category I", "Infrastructure fund under Category I", "Hedge fund under Category III", "Fund of funds under Category II"], 0, "Special situation funds invest in special situation assets such as stressed loans and securities of stressed companies and are a Category I sub-category."], ["Valuation", "Why might an AIF apply a discount for lack of control to a minority stake?", ["A minority holder cannot direct company decisions", "Minority stakes are always worth more than controlling stakes", "SEBI requires a fixed 50% discount on all minority stakes", "Minority stakes cannot be valued at all"], 0, "Without control, the investor cannot set strategy, dividends or a sale, which can reduce the value per share compared with a controlling stake. There is no fixed regulatory discount."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiation – Investor Perspective", "Under SEBI's framework on investor rights in AIFs, differential rights granted to select investors must:", ["Be kept secret from other investors", "Not harm the rights of other investors", "Always include higher returns guaranteed by the sponsor", "Be approved by the stock exchange"], 1, "SEBI requires pro-rata rights generally, permitting differential rights only within its framework and without affecting other investors adversely. Guaranteed returns are not permitted."], ["Taxation – India Specific", "Management fees charged by an AIF manager attract:", ["Goods and Services Tax", "Securities Transaction Tax", "Commodity Transaction Tax", "No indirect tax"], 0, "Management fees are a supply of services and attract GST, which increases the cost borne by the fund. STT and CTT apply to securities and commodity transactions."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "Why is an IPO often an attractive exit for a PE fund?", ["It guarantees the fund can sell its entire stake on listing day", "It eliminates lock-in requirements on pre-IPO shareholders", "It may achieve a high valuation and liquidity", "It avoids SEBI disclosure rules"], 2, "Listing can deliver a strong valuation and a liquid market, but pre-IPO shareholders face lock-ins and full sale is rarely immediate."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "Which practice best supports good governance in a fund's investment decisions?", ["Approvals given orally by the chief investment officer alone, without any written record", "Rotating approval rights among junior analysts", "Allowing investee companies to vote on fund decisions", "Documented IC minutes with reasons for approval"], 3, "Written records of IC deliberations and reasons create accountability and an audit trail. Oral approvals by one person weaken governance."], ["AIF Regulatory Framework", "A Category II AIF has investable funds of ₹240 crore. Under the general concentration limit, the maximum it can invest in one investee company is:", ["₹24 crore", "₹60 crore", "₹48 crore", "₹120 crore"], 1, "Category I and II AIFs may invest up to 25% of investable funds in a single investee company (large value funds have a higher limit). 25% × 240 = ₹60 crore."], ["Alternative Investment Funds in India", "Which fund is NOT covered by the SEBI AIF Regulations?", ["A private equity fund pooling money from HNIs", "A venture debt fund raising ₹200 crore privately", "A mutual fund registered under SEBI's mutual fund regulations", "A long-short fund for sophisticated investors"], 2, "Mutual funds, collective investment schemes and certain other regulated vehicles are excluded from the AIF definition. Privately pooled PE, debt and hedge-style funds fall within it."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "Which question best tests whether a manager's track record is attributable to the current team?", ["What were the overall market returns in those years?", "How many offices does the manager have?", "Which current team members led the past deals?", "What is the manager's brand recognition among retail investors?"], 2, "Attribution analysis checks whether the people who generated past returns are still with the firm. Market returns and branding do not show who created value."], ["Alternative Investment Funds in India", "Angel funds in India are a sub-category of:", ["Category II AIF private equity funds investing in late-stage companies", "Category III AIF", "Category I AIF (venture capital funds)", "Portfolio management services"], 2, "Angel funds are a sub-category of venture capital funds under Category I, pooling money from angel investors for start-ups. They are not PMS or Category III."], ["Alternative Investment Fund Structuring", "In a trust-based AIF, who is the 'settlor'?", ["The person who creates the trust with an initial settlement", "The investor with the largest commitment", "The custodian holding the assets", "The merchant banker filing the PPM"], 0, "The settlor creates the trust by settling an initial sum and executing the trust deed, usually the sponsor. Investors become contributors or beneficiaries through contribution agreements."], ["Taxation – India Specific", "Why do Category I and II AIFs issue investors an annual statement of income allocated, by nature of income?", ["Because investors cannot see the fund's NAV otherwise", "So investors can report each income type", "To claim GST credits", "Because SEBI fixes investors' tax rates"], 1, "With pass-through, investors are taxed on their share of each income type — capital gains, interest, dividends — so the AIF must report the breakdown for correct filing."], ["Concepts in the AIF Industry", "The 'investment period' of a close-ended AIF is the period during which:", ["Investors can redeem their units at NAV on demand", "Only exits are allowed and no new investments are made", "The manager can make new portfolio investments", "The fund raises capital from new investors every month without a final close"], 2, "During the investment period the manager deploys commitments into new deals. After it ends, the fund mainly manages and exits existing investments."], ["Alternative Investment Fund Structuring", "An AIF offers two classes of units: Class A for investors and Class B for the manager team, which carries entitlement to carried interest. Class B units are used mainly to:", ["Channel carried interest to the manager team", "Give the manager team voting control over all investor decisions", "Avoid the minimum investment requirement for investors", "Allow the manager to redeem units at any time"], 0, "Separate unit classes allow carried interest and different fee terms to be allocated to the manager team, as disclosed in the PPM. They do not give overriding control or waive investor requirements."], ["AIF Regulatory Framework", "Which grievance redressal route is available to an investor with an unresolved complaint against an AIF manager?", ["The Insurance Ombudsman", "The NPS Central Grievance Management System", "SEBI SCORES, followed by online dispute resolution if needed", "The Banking Ombudsman of RBI for all investment complaints"], 2, "AIF investors can lodge complaints on SEBI's SCORES and, if unresolved, use the online dispute resolution mechanism for securities market disputes."], ["Concepts in the AIF Industry", "A fund generates proceeds of ₹160 crore on ₹100 crore of capital. The hurdle amount is ₹30 crore and carry is 20% of profits above the hurdle with no catch-up. The manager's carry is:", ["₹12 crore", "₹8 crore", "₹30 crore", "₹6 crore"], 3, "Profit = ₹60 crore. Profit above hurdle = 60 − 30 = ₹30 crore. Carry = 20% × 30 = ₹6 crore. ₹12 crore applies 20% to the whole profit, as a full catch-up would."], ["Alternative Investment Fund Structuring", "In a unified structure, foreign investors invest:", ["Only through an offshore fund that invests via the FPI route", "Only in listed Indian shares through a mutual fund", "Through an Indian bank fixed deposit", "Directly into the Indian AIF alongside domestic investors"], 3, "In a unified structure, offshore and domestic investors invest directly into the same Indian AIF, sometimes using an offshore pooling vehicle. In contrast, a pure offshore structure uses FPI or FDI routes."], ["Concepts in the AIF Industry", "The 'vintage year' of a private equity fund usually refers to:", ["The year the fund manager was incorporated as a company", "The year the fund made its first investment or capital call", "The year the fund is expected to wind up", "The year the fund's last investment is exited"], 1, "Vintage year marks the start of a fund's investing activity and is used to compare funds that faced similar market conditions. It is not the manager's founding or the fund's end."], ["Good Practices", "A distributor keeps records of risk-profiling and suitability assessments for each AIF client. This mainly helps to:", ["Avoid paying GST on distribution fees", "Increase the fund's IRR", "Bypass the minimum investment", "Show the advice was appropriate"], 3, "Records demonstrate that the distributor assessed suitability before recommending and are required for compliance and dispute resolution."], ["Taxation – India Specific", "Capital gains earned by a Category II AIF on selling unlisted shares are taxed in investors' hands as:", ["Business income in all cases", "Income from other sources", "Capital gains, with the same character", "Exempt income"], 2, "Under the pass-through regime, income other than business income keeps its character in investors' hands, so capital gains remain capital gains."], ["Alternative Investment Funds in India", "A fund that invests in units of other Category I and II AIFs, without leverage, would normally be registered as:", ["Category II AIF (fund of funds)", "Category I AIF as a social impact fund", "Category III AIF", "A mutual fund under SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations"], 0, "Funds of AIFs are generally registered as Category II AIFs. Category III is for funds using complex strategies and leverage, and mutual funds have a separate framework."], ["AIF Regulatory Framework", "The minimum tenure of a Category I or II AIF is:", ["Three years", "One year", "Five years", "Ten years"], 0, "Category I and II AIFs must be close-ended with a minimum tenure of three years from the final close. Category III may be open-ended or close-ended."], ["Alternative Investment Funds in India", "A debt fund that lends to unlisted mid-sized companies without leverage would typically register as:", ["Category I AIF as an SME fund", "Category III AIF", "Category II AIF", "A non-banking financial company"], 2, "A private debt fund without leverage or special incentives is a Category II AIF. Category III is for complex trading strategies, and NBFCs are regulated by the RBI under a different framework."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "Private credit funds typically earn returns by:", ["Buying listed shares and holding them for dividends", "Investing only in gold and silver", "Lending to companies and earning interest and fees", "Holding cash in bank deposits"], 2, "Private credit funds lend directly to companies, earning interest, fees and sometimes equity kickers. They are a form of debt investment, not listed equity or commodities."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "A GP-led continuation vehicle typically gives existing investors:", ["A compulsory transfer of all their units to the new vehicle", "A choice to sell or roll over into the new vehicle", "A guaranteed premium over NAV", "The right to manage the continuation vehicle"], 1, "In a continuation fund, selected assets move to a new vehicle; existing investors can usually sell for cash or roll their interest. Fairness requires an independent price process."], ["AIF Regulatory Framework", "SEBI requires every AIF scheme to offer a 'direct plan'. Its main feature is:", ["A guaranteed minimum return", "Shorter lock-in than the regular plan", "A lower minimum investment of ₹10 lakh", "No distribution or placement fee is charged"], 3, "A direct plan is for investors coming directly or through SEBI-registered investment advisers or PMS and carries no distribution fee. It has the same lock-in and minimums."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiation – Investor Perspective", "A 'for-cause removal' clause allows investors to:", ["Remove the manager for events like fraud or breach", "Redeem their units at NAV whenever they wish", "Appoint a new auditor without any reason", "Veto every individual investment"], 0, "For-cause removal lets investors replace the manager following defined events such as fraud, gross negligence or material breach, usually by a specified majority."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiation – Investor Perspective", "An investor fails to fund a drawdown notice. The contribution agreement typically allows the manager to:", ["Raise the commitments of all other investors without consent", "Cancel the fund automatically", "Waive the default without any consequences in all cases", "Apply default penalties to the defaulting investor"], 3, "Default clauses permit penalties such as interest, forfeiture of a portion of the interest or forced transfer, protecting non-defaulting investors from funding gaps."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "The extra return investors expect for locking up capital in long-dated private funds is known as the:", ["Illiquidity premium", "Equity risk premium on large-cap listed shares", "Term premium on government bonds", "Currency premium"], 0, "Investors demand compensation for being unable to sell easily; this is the illiquidity premium. The equity risk premium relates to listed equity versus risk-free assets."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "An investor in a 10-year AIF needs liquidity in year five. A realistic option is to:", ["Redeem units from the fund at NAV on request", "Ask SEBI to cancel its commitment", "Stop paying capital calls without consequences", "Sell its interest on the secondary market"], 3, "Close-ended AIFs do not offer redemption; investors needing liquidity can sell their interests to secondary buyers, typically at a discount and with manager consent."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "Which document defines the fund's investment strategy, which the investment process must follow?", ["The custodian agreement", "The KYC form of the investor", "The private placement memorandum", "The investee company's articles of association"], 2, "The PPM sets out the strategy, restrictions and terms; investments must conform to it. Material deviations require investor consent."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "Venture capital differs from buyout private equity mainly because venture capital:", ["Acquires controlling stakes in mature companies using significant debt financing", "Invests only in listed companies", "Backs early-stage firms with minority stakes", "Avoids technology businesses"], 2, "Venture capital funds early-stage, often loss-making companies and usually takes minority stakes. Buyouts acquire control of mature businesses, typically with leverage."], ["Valuation", "Under SEBI's framework, the valuation of AIF investments must be done by an independent valuer. 'Independent' mainly means the valuer:", ["Is a large investor in the fund", "Is the fund's own investment committee", "Is appointed by the investee company", "Is not an associate of the manager or sponsor"], 3, "Independence requires the valuer to be free from relationships with the manager, sponsor and trustee that could bias valuations. Investors or investees cannot provide unbiased values."], ["Alternative Investment Funds in India", "Which feature is allowed for Category III AIFs but not for Category I and II AIFs?", ["Investment in unlisted companies of any kind, including early-stage start-ups", "Charging a management fee", "Leverage for investment purposes", "Raising money from accredited investors"], 2, "Category III AIFs can use leverage, within SEBI limits, to make investments. Category I and II AIFs may borrow only temporarily for operational needs, and all categories can charge fees."], ["Risk and Return – Fund and Investor Perspective", "Using the same fund (paid-in ₹80 crore, distributions ₹60 crore, NAV ₹52 crore), the RVPI is:", ["0.75x", "1.40x", "0.87x", "0.65x"], 3, "RVPI = residual value ÷ paid-in = 52 ÷ 80 = 0.65x. It shows how much value remains unrealised in the portfolio."], ["Taxation – India Specific", "Business income earned by a Category I AIF trust is taxed:", ["At the fund level", "Only in investors' hands as exempt income", "As capital gains in investors' hands", "At a concessional rate of 1%"], 0, "Business income is excluded from pass-through and is taxed at the fund level; distributions of that income to investors are then exempt in their hands."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "A due diligence questionnaire (DDQ) is mainly used to:", ["Replace the PPM as the legal offer document", "Calculate the fund's NAV", "Collect standard information about the manager", "Register the investor as an accredited investor"], 2, "A DDQ gathers consistent information on strategy, team, operations, compliance and terms, making comparison across managers easier. It does not replace the PPM."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiation – Investor Perspective", "A 'GP giveback' provision allows the manager to:", ["Return carried interest to investors each year", "Recall distributions to meet fund indemnities", "Give back unused commitments after the first close", "Refund management fees on demand"], 1, "A giveback lets the fund recall part of past distributions from investors to meet indemnity obligations or liabilities. It differs from a clawback, which recovers excess carry from the manager."], ["Valuation", "The 'comparable transactions' method values a company using:", ["Book value of the company's fixed assets", "Prices paid in recent acquisitions of similar firms", "The company's share capital", "The fund's original cost"], 1, "Precedent transaction multiples reflect what buyers have paid for similar businesses, often including a control premium, unlike trading multiples of listed peers."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "A Category II AIF wants to buy shares from another scheme of the same manager. This is generally:", ["Prohibited outright without exception", "A conflict needing approval as per regulations", "Permitted freely as both schemes are managed by the same entity", "Allowed only if the shares are listed"], 1, "Transactions between schemes of the same manager raise conflicts; SEBI requires disclosure and investor consent as prescribed, plus fair valuation. They are not automatically prohibited."]]};
